---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import BrowserMock from '../components/BrowserMock.astro';
import PhoneMock from '../components/PhoneMock.astro';

// Linked from airline-request emails, so the copy is written for crew who are wary
// of being asked to send anything from a company portal. The computer route is the
// developer panel only (Copy outerHTML, computer layout then phone layout): no portal
// can block it, so nobody is asked twice. Checked against a real roster page
// (2026-10-30): no password, token, session or cookie values in the copy. It does
// contain crew and hotel names, and the page says so. Known gap: outerHTML of <html>
// leaves out iframe documents, so a portal that frames its roster needs a follow-up.
//
// The phone way (view-source: + Print/PDF) saves the page's original HTML, before
// scripts run. Portals that load the roster after the page opens (Pegasus fills
// #updatedSearchResult from a script) will come out without duties, hence the caveat.
// Browser labels come from public guides, not device testing: Samsung Internet
// ≡ → Print/PDF and Desktop version; Chrome ⋮ → Share → Print and Desktop site.
// Whether current Chrome for Android still honours view-source: is unconfirmed.
// Inline tags stay on the same line as their neighbouring text: Astro drops a line
// break next to an inline tag, gluing the words together.

const email = 'info@rosternado.com';
const mailSubject = 'Roster page for my airline';
const mailBody = [
  'Airline:',
  'Roster website address:',
  'Email I use in Rosternado (if I have an account):',
  '',
  'Attached: my roster files and a screenshot.',
  '',
].join('\n');
const mailto = `mailto:${email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent(mailSubject)}&body=${encodeURIComponent(mailBody)}`;

const attachments = ['roster-desktop.txt · 660 KB', 'roster-phone.txt · 650 KB', 'Screenshot.png'];
---

<Layout
  title="Add your airline — Rosternado"
  description="How to send your crew roster page so Rosternado can build an import for your airline: what the saved files contain, what they don't, and what happens to them."
>
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow page">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Add your airline</p>
      <h1>Help bring Rosternado to your airline</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        To build the roster import for your airline, I need two copies of your roster page.
        It takes about five minutes on a computer, and your password stays with you.
        No computer? There's an <a href="#phone">Android phone way</a> further down.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="why" aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">Why your roster page, not a file</h2>
      <p class="prose">Rosternado doesn't import roster files. It reads your roster straight from your airline's crew website, inside the app. There's nothing to download or export, and no file to hunt for on your phone.</p>
      <ol class="flow">
        <li><span>1</span>Open your crew website in the app</li>
        <li><span>2</span>Log in</li>
        <li><span>3</span>Tap Save</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="prose">To make that work for your airline, the app needs to know how your roster page is built: where every flight number, time and airport sits. That's what the copies below show me. It's also why a PDF or calendar export of your roster won't help. It shows your duties, but not how the page itself is built.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card card--text control" aria-labelledby="control">
      <h2 id="control">You stay in control</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li><strong>No password.</strong> I never ask for it, and a copied page doesn't contain it.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>No logging in.</strong> I never sign in to your airline's systems. You do
          everything yourself, in your own browser.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Nothing to install.</strong> No apps, no extensions, and nobody sees or controls your screen.</li>
        <li><strong>Just the roster page.</strong> Nothing from your email, payslips or any other page.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="computer">
      <p class="eyebrow">Recommended</p>
      <h2 id="computer">On a computer</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        You'll copy your roster page twice from your browser's developer panel: once as a
        computer shows it, and once in its phone layout. The app opens your portal in its
        phone layout, and some portals build that differently, so I need both.
      </p>

      <ol class="how">
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Open your roster</h3>
          <p>
            Log in to your crew portal in <strong>Chrome</strong> or <strong>Edge</strong> on a
            computer, and open your roster with a full month showing, the way you normally look
            at it.
          </p>
          <BrowserMock
            class="mock"
            url="crew.example-air.com/roster"
            label="Illustration: a crew portal for a made-up airline, Example Air, showing a roster table with flights, a day off and a standby."
          >
            <div class="portal">
              <div class="portal__head">
                <span class="portal__brand">EXAMPLE AIR</span>
                <span>Crew Portal</span>
                <span class="portal__user">A. Pilot</span>
              </div>
              <div class="portal__body">
                <p class="portal__month">November 2026</p>
                <table class="portal__table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr><th>Date</th><th>Duty</th><th>Flight</th><th>From</th><th>To</th></tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr><td>03 Nov</td><td><span class="code">FLT</span></td><td>EX101</td><td>IST 06:10</td><td>LHR 08:55</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>03 Nov</td><td><span class="code">FLT</span></td><td>EX102</td><td>LHR 10:05</td><td>IST 15:40</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>04 Nov</td><td><span class="code">OFF</span></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
                    <tr><td>05 Nov</td><td><span class="code">STBY</span></td><td></td><td>05:00</td><td>17:00</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
          </BrowserMock>
        </li>

        <li class="step">
          <h3>Open the developer panel</h3>
          <p>Press <kbd>F12</kbd> on Windows, or <kbd>⌘ Cmd</kbd> + <kbd>Option</kbd> + <kbd>I</kbd> on a Mac. A panel opens beside the page.</p>
          <p>
            It looks technical, but it's built into every browser, it only reads the page you're
            already looking at, and closing it changes nothing.
          </p>
          <p class="note">Use Chrome or Edge, which are both free. Safari and Firefox lay the panel out differently.</p>
        </li>

        <li class="step">
          <h3>Copy the computer layout</h3>
          <ol class="substeps">
            <li>Click <strong>Elements</strong> at the top of the panel.</li>
            <li>Right-click the very first line, which starts with <code>&lt;html</code>, and choose <strong>Copy → Copy outerHTML</strong>.</li>
            <li>Open Notepad (Windows) or TextEdit (Mac). In TextEdit, choose <strong>Format → Make Plain Text</strong> first. Paste, and save it as <strong>roster-desktop.txt</strong>.</li>
          </ol>

          <BrowserMock
            id="copy-outerhtml"
            class="mock mock--wide"
            url="crew.example-air.com/roster"
            label="Illustration: in the developer panel's Elements tab, the first line, html lang equals en, is selected. A right-click menu is open with Copy, and its submenu has Copy outerHTML highlighted."
          >
            <div class="dt dt--copy">
              <div class="dt__page dt__page--ghost"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
              <div class="dt__panel">
                <div class="dt__tools">
                  <span class="dt__icon">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 2h9v5M2 2v9h5M8 8l6 2.5-2.5 1L10.5 14z"></path></svg>
                  </span>
                  <span class="dt__icon">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="1.5" y="3" width="9" height="11" rx="1"></rect><rect x="11.5" y="6" width="3.5" height="8" rx="0.8"></rect></svg>
                  </span>
                  <span class="dt__tab dt__tab--on">Elements</span>
                  <span class="dt__tab">Console</span>
                </div>
                <div class="dt__tree">
                  <div>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</div>
                  <div class="dt__sel"><span class="t">&lt;html</span> <span class="a">lang</span>=<span class="v">"en"</span><span class="t">&gt;</span></div>
                  <div class="i">▸ <span class="t">&lt;head&gt;</span>…<span class="t">&lt;/head&gt;</span></div>
                  <div class="i">▸ <span class="t">&lt;body&gt;</span>…<span class="t">&lt;/body&gt;</span></div>
                  <div><span class="t">&lt;/html&gt;</span></div>
                </div>
                <div class="ctx">
                  <div class="ctx__menu">
                    <span>Add attribute</span>
                    <span>Edit as HTML</span>
                    <span class="ctx__on">Copy ▸</span>
                    <span>Hide element</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="ctx__menu ctx__menu--sub">
                    <span>Copy element</span>
                    <span class="ctx__pick">Copy outerHTML</span>
                    <span>Copy selector</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </BrowserMock>
        </li>

        <li class="step">
          <h3>Copy the phone layout</h3>
          <ol class="substeps">
            <li>Click the <strong>phone-and-tablet icon</strong> at the top-left of the panel, or press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>M</kbd> (<kbd>⌘ Cmd</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>M</kbd> on a Mac). The page shrinks to phone size.</li>
            <li>Reload the page with <kbd>F5</kbd> (<kbd>⌘ Cmd</kbd> + <kbd>R</kbd> on a Mac). If your roster disappeared, open it again.</li>
            <li>Copy it the same way as in <a href="#copy-outerhtml">step 3</a>, into a new plain-text file, and save it as <strong>roster-phone.txt</strong>.</li>
          </ol>

          <BrowserMock
            class="mock mock--wide"
            url="crew.example-air.com/roster"
            label="Illustration: the developer panel is open beside the roster page. The phone-and-tablet icon at the top-left of the panel is highlighted, and the roster now shows in a narrow phone-sized frame labelled iPhone 12 Pro, 390 by 844."
          >
            <div class="dt">
              <div class="dt__page">
                <div class="dt__device">iPhone 12 Pro ▾ <span>390 × 844</span></div>
                <div class="dt__phone">
                  <div class="dt__phonehead">EXAMPLE AIR</div>
                  <div class="dt__card"><b>03 Nov</b> EX101<br />IST 06:10 → LHR 08:55</div>
                  <div class="dt__card"><b>03 Nov</b> EX102<br />LHR 10:05 → IST 15:40</div>
                  <div class="dt__card"><b>04 Nov</b> Day off</div>
                </div>
                <span class="badge badge--reload">2</span>
                <span class="dt__reload">⟳ Reload</span>
              </div>
              <div class="dt__panel">
                <div class="dt__tools">
                  <span class="dt__icon">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 2h9v5M2 2v9h5M8 8l6 2.5-2.5 1L10.5 14z"></path></svg>
                  </span>
                  <span class="dt__icon dt__icon--pick">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="1.5" y="3" width="9" height="11" rx="1"></rect><rect x="11.5" y="6" width="3.5" height="8" rx="0.8"></rect></svg>
                    <span class="badge">1</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="dt__tab dt__tab--on">Elements</span>
                  <span class="dt__tab">Console</span>
                  <span class="dt__tab">Sources</span>
                </div>
                <div class="dt__tree">
                  <div>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</div>
                  <div><span class="t">&lt;html</span> <span class="a">lang</span>=<span class="v">"en"</span><span class="t">&gt;</span></div>
                  <div class="i">▸ <span class="t">&lt;head&gt;</span>…<span class="t">&lt;/head&gt;</span></div>
                  <div class="i">▸ <span class="t">&lt;body&gt;</span>…<span class="t">&lt;/body&gt;</span></div>
                  <div><span class="t">&lt;/html&gt;</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </BrowserMock>
        </li>

        <li class="step">
          <h3>Email them to me</h3>
          <p>
            Send <strong>roster-desktop.txt</strong> and <strong>roster-phone.txt</strong> to <a href={mailto}>{email}</a>, together with a screenshot of your roster page so I can see what it looks like for you. Tell me your airline and the address of its roster website.
          </p>
          <BrowserMock
            class="mock"
            title="New message"
            label={`Illustration: an email to ${email} with three attachments: roster-desktop.txt, roster-phone.txt and Screenshot.png.`}
          >
            <div class="mail">
              <div class="mail__row"><span>To</span>{email}</div>
              <div class="mail__row"><span>Subject</span>{mailSubject}</div>
              <div class="mail__text">
                Airline: Example Air<br />Roster website: crew.example-air.com
              </div>
              <div class="mail__files">
                {
                  attachments.map((name) => (
                    <span class="file">
                      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M21 11.5 12.6 19.9a5.5 5.5 0 0 1-7.8-7.8l8.5-8.5a3.7 3.7 0 0 1 5.2 5.2l-8.5 8.5a1.8 1.8 0 0 1-2.6-2.6L15.2 7" /></svg>
                      {name}
                    </span>
                  ))
                }
              </div>
            </div>
          </BrowserMock>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="phone">
      <h2 id="phone">No computer? On an Android phone</h2>

      <div class="caveat">
        <p><strong>A computer is still the better way.</strong> A phone browser can only save the page's original code, from before the page fills itself in. Many portals load your duties a moment after the page opens (for example when you press Search), and then the phone files won't include them. If I can't build the import from them, I'll ask for the computer files.</p>
        <p><strong>iPhone:</strong> iPhone browsers can't show page code, so please use a computer.</p>
        <p><strong>Browsers differ.</strong> The steps are written for Samsung Internet, with Chrome's labels where they differ. Some browsers, including newer Chrome versions, may not show the code at all. If yours doesn't, use Samsung Internet or a computer.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="how how--phone">
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Open your roster</h3>
          <p>Log in and open your roster in your phone's browser, the way you normally look at it.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Show the page's code</h3>
          <p>Tap the address bar and type <code>view-source:</code> in front of the address, so it reads <code>view-source:https://…</code> Then tap <strong>Go</strong>. The page turns into code.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Save it as a PDF</h3>
          <p>Open the browser menu. In Samsung Internet tap <strong>≡ → Print/PDF</strong>. In Chrome tap <strong>⋮ → Share → Print</strong>. Choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong> as the printer, name it <strong>roster-phone</strong> and save.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Do it again in desktop view</h3>
          <p>Go back to your roster and open the menu again. In Samsung Internet tap <strong>Desktop version</strong>. In Chrome tick <strong>Desktop site</strong>. Then repeat steps 2 and 3, and name this file <strong>roster-desktop</strong>.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="step">
          <h3>Email them to me</h3>
          <p>Send both PDFs to <a href={mailto}>{email}</a> with a screenshot of your roster, and tell me your airline and its roster website.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="phones">
        <figure>
          <PhoneMock
            url="https://crew.example-air.com/roster"
            prefix="view-source:"
            label="Illustration: a phone browser's address bar reading view-source: followed by the roster address, with the keyboard's Go key highlighted."
          >
            <div class="ph-ghost"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
            <div class="ph-keys">
              <div class="ph-keys__row"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
              <div class="ph-keys__row"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
              <div class="ph-keys__row"><i class="w"></i><i class="ph-go">Go</i></div>
            </div>
          </PhoneMock>
          <figcaption><span>2</span>Type view-source: in front</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <PhoneMock
            url="https://crew.example-air.com/roster"
            prefix="view-source:"
            label="Illustration: the page now shows numbered lines of code, and the browser menu is open with Print/PDF highlighted."
          >
            <div class="ph-code">
              <div><b>1</b>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</div>
              <div><b>2</b>&lt;html lang="en"&gt;</div>
              <div><b>3</b>&lt;head&gt;</div>
              <div><b>4</b>&lt;title&gt;Crew&lt;/title&gt;</div>
              <div><b>5</b>&lt;/head&gt;</div>
              <div><b>6</b>&lt;body&gt;</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ph-sheet">
              <span>Add page to</span>
              <span>Find on page</span>
              <span>Desktop version</span>
              <span class="ph-pick">Print/PDF</span>
            </div>
          </PhoneMock>
          <figcaption><span>3</span>Menu → Print/PDF</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <PhoneMock label="Illustration: the print screen with the printer set to Save as PDF and a PDF button to save the file.">
            <div class="ph-print">
              <div class="ph-print__bar"><span class="ph-pick">Save as PDF ▾</span></div>
              <div class="ph-print__page">
                <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
              </div>
              <span class="ph-print__btn">PDF</span>
            </div>
          </PhoneMock>
          <figcaption><span>3</span>Save as PDF</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <PhoneMock
            url="https://crew.example-air.com/roster"
            label="Illustration: back on the roster page with the browser menu open and Desktop version highlighted."
          >
            <div class="ph-roster">
              <div class="ph-roster__head">EXAMPLE AIR</div>
              <div class="ph-roster__row"><b>03 Nov</b> EX101 IST → LHR</div>
              <div class="ph-roster__row"><b>03 Nov</b> EX102 LHR → IST</div>
            </div>
            <div class="ph-sheet">
              <span>Add page to</span>
              <span>Find on page</span>
              <span class="ph-pick">Desktop version</span>
              <span>Print/PDF</span>
            </div>
          </PhoneMock>
          <figcaption><span>4</span>Menu → Desktop version</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block" aria-labelledby="inside">
      <h2 id="inside">What's inside the files</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        Each file is your roster page's code: a .txt from a computer, or a .pdf from a phone.
        It holds exactly what you see on screen, plus
        the layout code that tells the browser how to draw it: table borders, fonts, colours.
        That layout is what I need. It shows me where your flight numbers, times and airports
        sit, so the import knows where to find them.
      </p>

      <figure class="excerpt">
        <pre><code><span class="tag">&lt;tr class="duty-row"&gt;</span>
<span class="tag">  &lt;td class="date"&gt;</span><mark>03 Nov</mark><span class="tag">&lt;/td&gt;</span>
<span class="tag">  &lt;td data-title="Flight No:"&gt;</span><mark>EX101</mark><span class="tag">&lt;/td&gt;</span>
<span class="tag">  &lt;td data-title="Departure:"&gt;</span><mark>IST 06:10</mark><span class="tag">&lt;/td&gt;</span>
<span class="tag">  &lt;td data-title="Arrival:"&gt;</span><mark>LHR 08:55</mark><span class="tag">&lt;/td&gt;</span>
<span class="tag">&lt;/tr&gt;</span></code></pre>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="legend legend--data">What you see on the page</span>
          <span class="legend legend--tag">Layout code that draws it</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="split">
        <div class="card card--text">
          <h3>In the files</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>Your duties, dates and times</li>
            <li>Flight numbers and airports</li>
            <li>Your name, and any crew or hotel names your roster lists</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card card--text">
          <h3>Not in the files</h3>
          <ul class="crosses">
            <li>Your password</li>
            <li>Your login. Your browser keeps it separately from the page, and if a portal ever tucks login details into its page code, I never use them.</li>
            <li>Anything from other pages or tabs</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="prose dim">
        I checked a copy of a real roster page: it held the roster and its layout, and no
        password or login details. Want to see for yourself? Open the .txt file before you
        send it. It's the same code your browser reads to draw your roster page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block card promise" aria-labelledby="after">
      <h2 id="after">What happens to them</h2>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>I'm the only one who opens them. Rosternado is built by one airline pilot.</li>
        <li>They're used only to build and test your airline's import.</li>
        <li>They're never shared, published or sold.</li>
        <li>I delete them as soon as your airline's import is built, or straight away if it turns out it can't be.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="thanks">
        When your airline's import goes live, I'll reply to your email, and you get <strong>12 months of Pro</strong> as a thank-you.
      </p>
      <p class="sign">İnanç, airline pilot and developer of Rosternado</p>
    </section>

    <section class="block faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
      <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <h3>Is this allowed by my airline?</h3>
        <p>
          It's your own schedule, the same page you can already print or screenshot. Some
          airlines have rules about sharing crew documents, so if you're unsure, check first.
          Sending is always optional, and you can add duties by hand in the meantime.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <h3>Why two files?</h3>
        <p>
          The app opens your portal in its phone layout. Some portals build their phone layout
          differently from the computer one, so I need both to make the import work in the app.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <h3>Why not just a screenshot?</h3>
        <p>
          A screenshot shows how your roster looks. The copied code shows where each piece of it
          sits, which is what the import reads. I ask for both.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <h3>Can I do this on my phone?</h3>
        <p>
          On an Android phone, yes: see <a href="#phone">the phone way</a>. A computer is still
          better, because a phone can only save the page's original code, which may not include
          your duties. iPhone browsers can't show page code, so on an iPhone please use a
          computer.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq__item">
        <h3>What happens next?</h3>
        <p>
          I build the import from your files, and I may ask a question or two by email. When
          it's ready, I'll ask you to try it in the app.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block cta-card">
      <h2>Ready to send them?</h2>
      <p>Email your files and a screenshot to <strong>{email}</strong>.</p>
      <a class="btn" href={mailto}>Write the email</a>
      <p class="cta-note">Attach the files yourself. Email links can't add attachments.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .block {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 2rem + 2vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .block > .eyebrow + h2 {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  /* Direct children only: paragraphs inside the light mock windows keep their own colours. */
  .prose,
  .step > p {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .prose + .prose,
  .step > p + p {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .prose + .how {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* ---------- Why not a file ---------- */

  .why {
